          "See how the pillars cover different failure points. If the report were excellent but the certificates were missing, the electronic evidence could be excluded. If the certificates were in order but the expert had no prepared answers, the cross-examination could go badly. If the testimony went well but the file was assembled late, the regulator could still act. Remove any one pillar and a gap opens.",
          "The example describes a prosecution in which every statement in the expert report is tied to an indexed working paper, a hash value and a certificate, and the file was assembled in the allowed window. The court accepts the testimony. We should read this as a model of preparation, not as a prediction for any real case. Courts decide on the facts, and a good file only ensures that the facts can be shown.",
          "The quiz states the benefit: grounding the testimony in verified, timely locked working papers gives strong proof that the evidence is intact and the expert is objective.",
          "Before the final day, take a breath and look back at the whole path. You learned to gather evidence, test it, report on it, collect electronic proof, defend it in court, and answer to the regulator. The next and last day brings it together in one capstone, where a single crisis tests all of these skills at the same time."
